<Layout
  title={`Footprints Without Feet | CBSE Class 10 English Supplementary | ${provider.name}`}
  description="Complete study notes, character motivations, themes, visual diagrams, and 20 CBSE Board MCQs for all chapters in Footprints Without Feet: A Triumph of Surgery, The Thief's Story, The Midnight Visitor, A Question of Trust, Griffin, The Making of a Scientist, The Necklace, Bholi, and The Book That Saved the Earth."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Footprints Without Feet
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · English Language &amp; Literature (Code 184) · Supplementary Reader</span>
      <h1>Footprints Without Feet: Complete Chapter Analysis &amp; Quiz</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Comprehensive coverage of all 9 supplementary reader chapters: character psychology, moral transformations, dramatic twists, irony, and 20 high-frequency CBSE Board Exam questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Footprints Without Feet: Complete Chapter Breakdown</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🐕 1. A Triumph of Surgery (James Herriot)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Blind Pampering vs. Practical Common Sense in Caregiving</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Patient:</strong> Tricki, a pampered Pekingese dog owned by the wealthy widow Mrs. Pumphrey, who feeds him malt, cod-liver oil, bowls of Horlicks, chocolates, and cakes. Tricki becomes grotesquely fat like a bloated sausage.</li>
            <li><strong>The Crisis:</strong> Tricki refuses to eat, suffers bouts of vomiting, and lies panting on his rug. Mrs. Pumphrey calls veterinary surgeon Dr. James Herriot.</li>
            <li><strong>The Surgery Cure:</strong> Dr. Herriot realizes surgery is unnecessary. He hospitalizes Tricki for two weeks, keeping him strictly on plenty of water and no food for two days, followed by unrestricted play and competition with a pack of rough greyhounds.</li>
            <li><strong>Mrs. Pumphrey's Gratitude:</strong> Tricki transforms into a lithe, hard-muscled animal. When Mrs. Pumphrey collects him, she weeps tears of joy and proclaims it "A Triumph of Surgery", unaware that common sense and exercise, not medical surgery, cured him.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🤝 2. The Thief's Story (Ruskin Bond)</h3>
          <p class="formula">The Transformative Power of Trust, Empathy &amp; Education</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Characters:</strong> Hari Singh, a 15-year-old seasoned thief who changes his name monthly to escape police; and Anil, an easygoing, kind 25-year-old freelance writer who earns by fits and starts.</li>
            <li><strong>The Relationship:</strong> Anil takes Hari in, promises to teach him to cook, write his name, add numbers, and write whole sentences. Hari appreciates the value of literacy ("Once I could write like an educated man, there would be no limit to what I could achieve").</li>
            <li><strong>The Theft:</strong> When Anil earns 600 rupees from selling a book to a publisher and tucks it under his mattress, Hari steals the bundle at night and runs to the railway station for the Lucknow Express.</li>
            <li><strong>The Moral Transformation:</strong> Standing on the deserted platform in pouring rain, Hari realizes that stealing 600 rupees makes him rich for a week, but learning to read and write would make him a truly respected, big man. He returns and slips the wet notes back under the mattress. The next morning, Anil hands him a damp 50-rupee note, declaring he will now be paid regularly, quietly acknowledging the event without reproach.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🕵️ 3. The Midnight Visitor (Robert Arthur)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Intellect &amp; Presence of Mind over Physical Weapons</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Subversion of Spy Tropes:</strong> Fowler, a romantic young writer, expects a glamorous secret agent like James Bond. Instead, Ausable is very fat, speaks French and German with an American accent, and lives in a gloomy sixth-floor room of a Parisian hotel.</li>
            <li><strong>The Intrusion:</strong> Turning on the room light, they discover Max, a slender rival secret agent, pointing a small automatic pistol, demanding the top-secret missile report Ausable is expecting.</li>
            <li><strong>The Fabricated Balcony:</strong> Without flinching, Ausable calmly complains about the hotel management, claiming a second person has entered through the nonexistent balcony beneath his window that month.</li>
            <li><strong>The Climax:</strong> A sudden knock on the door sounds. Ausable casually claims it is the police he requested for extra protection. Panicked, Max steps out of the window onto the "balcony" and plunges six stories to his death. The door opens to reveal Henry the waiter with drinks—there was no police, and no balcony had ever existed!</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🗝️ 4. A Question of Trust (Victor Canning)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Honor Among Thieves · Outsmarted by a Superior Mastermind</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Horace Danby:</strong> A 50-year-old respectable, unmarried locksmith who runs a successful business. However, he is not completely honest: once every year, he robs a wealthy house meticulously to purchase rare and expensive books through an agent.</li>
            <li><strong>The Heist at Shotover Grange:</strong> Horace plans to rob jewels worth 15,000 pounds from Shotover Grange while the family is in London and servants at the cinema. While cracking the safe, his hay fever triggers sneezing due to a bowl of flowers.</li>
            <li><strong>The Encounter:</strong> A young, poised, pretty woman in red appears, quieting the house dog Sherry. She speaks with authority, claiming to be the mistress of the house who forgot the safe combination. She offers not to report Horace if he cracks the safe for her.</li>
            <li><strong>The Betrayal:</strong> Horace opens the safe with his bare hands (without gloves) to please her. Two days later, police arrest Horace because his fingerprints are found all over the safe. The real mistress is a sharp-tongued 60-year-old woman; the young lady was a fellow jewel thief who brilliantly used him to commit the theft!</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>👣 5. Footprints without Feet (H.G. Wells)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Scientific Genius Divorced from Morality and Social Conscience</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Discovery:</strong> Griffin, a brilliant but lawless scientist, swallows rare drugs that make his human body as transparent as sheet of glass. When his landlord attempts to evict him, Griffin takes revenge by setting the house on fire and becoming an invisible wanderer in freezing London.</li>
            <li><strong>London Misadventures:</strong> Invisible and shivering, he slips into a big London department store for food and warm clothes. Next morning, chased by shop assistants, he strips naked into invisibility again. He burglarizes a theatrical company shop in Drury Lane, stealing bandages, fake nose, spectacles, and whiskers to give himself a visible face.</li>
            <li><strong>The Terror at Iping:</strong> Griffin arrives at Mrs. Hall's inn in the village of Iping. He locks himself in his room, conducts bizarre chemical experiments, and steals money from the clergyman's desk.</li>
            <li><strong>The Confrontation:</strong> When Mrs. Hall confronts him, Griffin rips off his whiskers and bandages, revealing a headless torso to the horrified villagers. Constable Jaffers arrives with a warrant to arrest "a man with or without a head." Griffin strips off his clothes one by one until he is entirely invisible, knocks Jaffers unconscious, and escapes.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🦋 6. The Making of a Scientist (Robert W. Peterson)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Inquisitive Curiosity · Scientific Rigour · Mother's Mentorship</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Childhood Curiosity:</strong> Richard Ebright, growing up in Reading, Pennsylvania, collects rocks, fossils, and all 25 species of butterflies found near his home by the second grade. His mother encourages him by buying telescopes, microscopes, cameras, and the life-changing book *The Travels of Monarch X*.</li>
            <li><strong>The Lesson of Failure:</strong> Entering the county science fair with frog tissue slides, he loses to competitors doing real experiments. He realizes science is not about neat displays, but about asking genuine questions and testing hypotheses.</li>
            <li><strong>Key Scientific Breakthroughs:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Monarch &amp; Viceroy:</em> Discovered that Viceroy butterflies mimic Monarchs because birds find Monarchs distasteful.
              <br/>• <em>The Gold Spots:</em> Disproved the belief that the 12 tiny gold spots on a Monarch pupa are ornamental; proved they produce a vital hormone necessary for the butterfly's full development.
              <br/>• <em>DNA Blueprint:</em> Identified the chemical structure of the hormone and hypothesized how cells read the blueprint of their DNA.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Qualities of a Scientist:</strong> A first-rate mind, curious intellect, and the will to win for the right reasons, complemented by diverse talents (champion debater, public speaker, and expert canoeist).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>💎 7. The Necklace (Guy de Maupassant)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Vanity &amp; Discontentment Leading to a Lifetime of Ruin</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Dissatisfied Soul:</strong> Mathilde Loisel, born into a family of clerks, dreams perpetually of aristocratic luxury, banquet halls, and costly tapestries while living in modest middle-class conditions with her devoted clerk husband.</li>
            <li><strong>The Ministry Ball:</strong> When her husband secures an invitation to the Minister of Public Instruction's grand ball, she buys a dress with his saved hunting money (400 francs) and borrows a dazzling diamond necklace from her wealthy friend Madame Jeanne Forestier. She is the center of admiration and beauty all evening.</li>
            <li><strong>The Tragic Loss:</strong> Returning home at 4:00 AM, Mathilde discovers the necklace is missing from her neck. Desperate searches yield nothing. Unable to confess the loss, the Loisels secretly buy an exact replacement diamond necklace for 36,000 francs.</li>
            <li><strong>A Decade of Squalor:</strong> To pay back borrowed loans at ruinous interest, they dismiss their maid, move into a garret, and work day and night for 10 grueling years. Mathilde turns into a rough, harsh, prematurely aged woman.</li>
            <li><strong>The Bitter Irony:</strong> Meeting Madame Forestier on the Champs-Élysées years later, Mathilde finally confesses the ordeal. Forestier gasps in pity: "Oh, my poor Mathilde! Mine was an imitation paste! It was worth at most five hundred francs!"</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌸 8. Bholi (K.A. Abbas)</h3>
          <p class="formula">The Dignity of the Girl Child · Education as Self-Actualization</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Neglected Child:</strong> Sulekha, called 'Bholi' (the simpleton), suffered a head injury at 10 months which impaired her brain, and a smallpox attack at age two which permanently disfigured her body with deep pockmarks. She stammered and was treated as a useless burden by her parents, Numberdar Ramlal and his wife.</li>
            <li><strong>The Sanctuary of School:</strong> Sent to the new village primary school only because the Tehsildar insisted on government officials setting an example, Bholi is terrified. However, her kind, smiling schoolteacher speaks to her gently, boosting her confidence and promising that with education, she will speak without stammering and earn everyone's respect.</li>
            <li><strong>The Dowry Demand:</strong> Years later, an unequal marriage is arranged with Bishamber Nath, a rich, lame, elderly grocer with grown-up children. Seeing Bholi's pockmarked face at the garland ceremony, Bishamber arrogantly refuses to marry her unless Ramlal pays 5,000 rupees in cash. Ramlal places his turban at the grocer's feet, weeping.</li>
            <li><strong>The Triumph:</strong> Once the money is placed before Bishamber and he steps forward with the garland, Bholi flings the garland into the sacred fire! Speaking with absolute clarity and calm dignity without a stammer, she refuses to marry a heartless, greedy coward. She declares she will take care of her parents in old age and teach in the very same school where she found her self-worth.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🪐 9. The Book That Saved the Earth (Claire Boiko)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Satirical Sci-Fi · Nursery Rhymes Defeating Martian Conquest</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Setting:</strong> 25th century, looking back at an attempted Martian invasion in the 20th century ("The Era of the Book").</li>
            <li><strong>The Invaders:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Think-Tank:</em> Commander-in-chief of Mars with a massive egg-shaped head, supreme arrogance, and childish vanity.
              <br/>• <em>Apprentice Noodle:</em> Humble, diplomatic subordinate who gently guides his foolish master.
              <br/>• <em>Space Probe Crew:</em> Captain Omega, Lieutenant Iota, and Sergeant Oop, landing in the Centerville Public Library on Earth.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Martian Misunderstandings:</strong> Unaware of what books are, Think-Tank first declares them "sandwiches" (Earthling communication devices for chewing). Later he guesses they are communication devices for listening to, and finally orders the crew to take intelligence vitamins to decode the written symbols.</li>
            <li><strong>The Mother Goose Panic:</strong> The crew reads the nursery rhyme book *Mother Goose*:
              <br/>• <em>Mistress Mary:</em> Thinks Earthlings have discovered how to grow silver bells and cockle shells in gardens, meaning they can bio-engineer rare metals and explosives.
              <br/>• <em>Hey Diddle Diddle:</em> Thinks Earth animals are space-trained ("the cow jumped over the moon").
              <br/>• <em>Humpty Dumpty:</em> Looking at the illustration of Humpty Dumpty, Think-Tank sees a dead ringer for his own balloon brain! Terrified that Earthlings are preparing to topple him and conquer Mars, he cancels the invasion, orders his crew to evacuate, and flees to Alpha Centauri 100 million miles away.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🚖 Reference: The Hack Driver (Sinclair Lewis)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Country Simplicity vs. Urban Intellectual Naivety</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Protagonist:</strong> An inexperienced young junior assistant clerk in a magnificent law firm whose distasteful job is serving legal summons to elusive witnesses.</li>
            <li><strong>The Assignment:</strong> Sent to the country town of New Mullion to serve a summons on Oliver Lutkins, an essential witness in a lawsuit.</li>
            <li><strong>Bill Magnuson (The Hack Driver):</strong> At the station, he meets a cheerful 40-year-old hack driver who offers to drive him all over town to track down Lutkins for two dollars an hour.</li>
            <li><strong>The Day-Long Deception:</strong> Bill takes the lawyer to Fritz's, Gustaff's barbershop, Gray's poolroom, and Lutkins's mother's farm—always entering first while warning the lawyer to stay behind. The lawyer falls in love with the friendly, wise country folk.</li>
            <li><strong>The Humiliation:</strong> Next day, returning with a colleague who knows Lutkins, the lawyer proudly points out Bill the hack driver. His colleague laughs out loud: "That isn't Bill, that is Oliver Lutkins himself!" The entire town, including Lutkins and his mother, gather to laugh at the outsmarted city lawyer.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Moral Trajectories &amp; Tragic Irony</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card visual-card">
          <span class="visual-badge">Comparative Moral Arc</span>
          <h3>Character Elevation: Bholi &amp; Hari Singh</h3>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect width="600" height="320" fill="#f8fafc" />
              
              <!-- Starting State (Degradation) -->
              <rect x="30" y="40" width="160" height="100" rx="8" fill="#fee2e2" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="110" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#991b1b">Initial State</text>
              <text x="110" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#7f1d1d">Bholi: Stammering, pockmarked</text>
              <text x="110" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#7f1d1d">Hari: 15-yr thief on the run</text>

              <!-- Catalyst (Education & Trust) -->
              <path d="M 190 90 L 230 90" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="3" />
              
              <rect x="230" y="40" width="160" height="100" rx="8" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="310" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#92400e">The Human Catalyst</text>
              <text x="310" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#78350f">Teacher: Gentleness &amp; patience</text>
              <text x="310" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#78350f">Anil: Unquestioned trust &amp; literacy</text>

              <!-- Culmination (Dignity) -->
              <path d="M 390 90 L 430 90" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="3" />

              <rect x="430" y="40" width="140" height="100" rx="8" fill="#dcfce7" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="500" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#166534">Transformed State</text>
              <text x="500" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#14532d">Bholi: Rejects dowry</text>
              <text x="500" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#14532d">Hari: Returns stolen 600₹</text>

              <!-- Synthesis Banner -->
              <rect x="50" y="180" width="500" height="110" rx="10" fill="#f0f9ff" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="300" y="210" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">Core Thematic Law of Footprints Without Feet</text>
              <text x="300" y="235" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#0c4a6e">Education and genuine empathy are not just academic or moral pursuits;</text>
              <text x="300" y="255" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#0c4a6e">they are tools of self-realization that liberate marginalized humans</text>
              <text x="300" y="275" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#0284c7">from criminal cycles (Hari) and societal subjugation (Bholi).</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Figure 1: How kindness and literacy construct unshakeable personal dignity in NCERT supplementary stories.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card visual-card">
          <span class="visual-badge">Plot Irony Breakdown</span>
          <h3>The Necklace: The Ruin of False Pretence</h3>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect width="600" height="320" fill="#f8fafc" />
              
              <!-- Left: The Borrowed Illusion -->
              <rect x="40" y="30" width="230" height="120" rx="8" fill="#fdf4ff" stroke="#c084fc" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="155" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#7e22ce">The 1-Night Illusion</text>
              <text x="155" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#581c87">Borrows diamond necklace</text>
              <text x="155" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#581c87">Triumph at Ministry Ball</text>
              <text x="155" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#a855f7">Real Cost: ~500 Francs</text>

              <!-- Right: The 10-Year Debt Reality -->
              <rect x="330" y="30" width="230" height="120" rx="8" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="445" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#991b1b">The 10-Year Penance</text>
              <text x="445" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#7f1d1d">Lost on way home</text>
              <text x="445" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#7f1d1d">Replaced with real gems</text>
              <text x="445" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#dc2626">Borrowed Cost: 36,000 Francs</text>

              <!-- Arrow pointing down -->
              <path d="M 155 150 L 155 190 L 300 190 L 300 210" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M 445 150 L 445 190 L 300 190" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2" />

              <!-- Bottom Outcome Box -->
              <rect x="50" y="210" width="500" height="90" rx="8" fill="#fffbeb" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="300" y="240" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#b45309">Maupassant's Stinging Satirical Twist</text>
              <text x="300" y="265" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#78350f">Mathilde sacrifices youth, health, and dignity for 10 years to repay a debt</text>
              <text x="300" y="285" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#92400e">for an imitation glass necklace that was never worth more than 500 francs!</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Figure 2: The economic and moral asymmetry that powers Guy de Maupassant's famous surprise ending.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quiz Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Practice: Footprints Without Feet</h2>
          <p class="muted">20 High-yield MCQs testing narrative twists, character motives, irony, and textual comprehension.</p>
        </div>
