  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  const questions = [
    {
      q: "What medical treatment did Dr. James Herriot administer to cure Tricki in 'A Triumph of Surgery'?",
      opts: [
        "A series of complex surgeries to remove abdominal fat",
        "No medicinal treatment; only controlled diet, abundant water, and energetic rough play with other dogs",
        "Daily injections of vitamins and expensive medicines imported from London",
        "A strict bed-rest regimen with sleeping pills"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Dr. Herriot gave Tricki no medical drugs whatsoever; simply cutting down his over-rich diet, giving him water, and letting him run with other dogs restored his health naturally."
    },
    {
      q: "In 'The Thief's Story', why did Hari Singh return to Anil's room after successfully stealing the 600 rupees?",
      opts: [
        "He forgot his train ticket to Lucknow",
        "The police surrounded the railway station",
        "He realized that literacy and education from Anil could make him a genuinely respected man, which was worth far more than stolen money",
        "He wanted to steal Anil's wristwatch as well"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      exp: "Standing on the station platform, Hari realized that money could only provide temporary, anxious luxury, while learning to write sentences could give him lasting dignity and respect."
    },
    {
      q: "How did Anil react the morning after the theft in 'The Thief's Story'?",
      opts: [
        "He called the police and had Hari arrested",
        "He gave Hari a still-damp 50-rupee note, promised regular wages, and never mentioned the theft",
        "He threw Hari out of his house in anger",
        "He deducted 600 rupees from Hari's future salary"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Anil gave Hari a damp 50-rupee note and promised he would now receive regular pay. He knew about the theft and return, but showed no anger, cementing Hari's reform."
    },
    {
      q: "In 'The Midnight Visitor', how did Ausable convince Max that a balcony existed outside his window?",
      opts: [
        "He showed Max forged architectural blueprints of the hotel",
        "He casually and convincingly complained that it was the second time that month someone had entered his room through that nuisance of a balcony",
        "He pointed to a curtain that fluttered in the breeze",
        "Fowler confirmed the presence of the balcony to save Ausable"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Ausable spoke with such natural irritation about the balcony belonging to the next apartment that Max believed him without checking."
    },
    {
      q: "Who was actually knocking at the door when Ausable claimed it was the police in 'The Midnight Visitor'?",
      opts: [
        "The hotel manager demanding room rent",
        "Henry the waiter, bringing the bottle of wine and two glasses Ausable had ordered",
        "Secret service commandos ready to arrest Max",
        "The French police commissioner"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "It was Henry the waiter bringing drinks. Ausable's swift invention that it was the police caused Max to leap to his death through the window."
    },
    {
      q: "What was Horace Danby's secret passion that drove him to commit one burglary every year in 'A Question of Trust'?",
      opts: [
        "Collecting expensive antique sports cars",
        "Buying rare and precious books through an undercover agent",
        "Financing luxury international vacations",
        "Gambling at horse races in London"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Horace Danby loved rare, expensive books and robbed a safe once every year to secretly purchase them via an agent."
    },
    {
      q: "How did the young woman in red trick Horace Danby at Shotover Grange?",
      opts: [
        "She held him at gunpoint until police arrived",
        "She posed convincingly as the lady of the house, getting him to open the safe without gloves to bypass her 'forgotten' code",
        "She offered to partner with him in future burglaries",
        "She called the servants back from the cinema"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Speaking with calm domestic authority and petting the dog Sherry, she posed as the mistress who had forgotten the code, tricking Horace into opening the safe with bare hands."
    },
    {
      q: "Why was Constable Jaffers flabbergasted when trying to arrest Griffin in 'Footprints without Feet'?",
      opts: [
        "Griffin turned into a wild beast",
        "He had to arrest a man who had removed his bandages, spectacles, and clothes, becoming completely headless and invisible",
        "Griffin offered him a massive bribe",
        "The magistrate cancelled the arrest warrant at the last moment"
      ],
      correct: 1,
